  "distributed_text": "In the late 1990s, volunteer computing emerged as a revolutionary paradigm that transformed idle personal computers into a massive, decentralized supercomputer. This era marked the transition from theoretical concepts to practical, large-scale scientific applications, driven by the rapid expansion of the internet and the increasing processing power of home PCs.\n\n### How It Worked: The Client-Server Model\n\nThe architecture of volunteer computing in the late 90s was relatively simple compared to modern standards, relying on a **client-server model** that leveraged the \"spare cycles\" of individual machines.\n\n1.  **The Software Client**: Users would download a small, dedicated software program (a screensaver or a background process) specific to a project. The most famous example was the **SETI@home** client, released in 1999.\n2.  **Work Unit Distribution**: When the user's computer was idle (or when the screensaver activated), the client software would contact a central server. The server would send a small packet of data, known as a **work unit**. For SETI@home, this was a snippet of radio telescope data; for other projects, it might be a segment of a protein folding simulation or a range of numbers to",
